{
  "title": "How a Microwave Heats Food",
  "screens": [
    {
      "title": "Hot edges, cold middle",
      "text": "You heat a bowl of leftovers, but the edges are steaming while the middle is still cool. Why doesn’t it all heat at once?",
      "imagePrompt": "A bowl of leftovers with steaming hot food around the edges and a visibly cooler center."
    },
    {
      "title": "Waves enter the food",
      "text": "Inside the oven, a device called a magnetron makes microwaves. These are invisible waves that bounce off the metal walls and enter your food.",
      "imagePrompt": "A cutaway microwave oven showing a magnetron, waves bouncing off the metal walls, and some waves entering a bowl of food."
    },
    {
      "title": "Water molecules get moving",
      "text": "Microwaves have an electric field that rapidly changes direction. It makes water molecules in the food try to turn back and forth. As they interact with nearby molecules, energy spreads through the food as heat.",
      "imagePrompt": "A close-up of water molecules in food turning back and forth as an electric field changes direction, with nearby molecules moving too."
    },
    {
      "title": "Some spots heat faster",
      "text": "Microwaves don’t reach every spot equally. The shape and thickness of food matter, and waves can be stronger in some places than others. That’s why the middle of a thick serving may stay cool.",
      "imagePrompt": "A thick serving of food in a microwave, with waves reaching different areas unevenly and a cooler middle."
    },
    {
      "title": "Stir and let it stand",
      "text": "Stirring brings cooler food toward hotter areas. Letting the food stand also gives heat time to spread from hotter spots to cooler ones. Follow the food’s heating and standing directions.",
      "imagePrompt": "A person stirring a bowl of heated leftovers, followed by the bowl resting on a counter as heat spreads through the food."
    }
  ],
  "check": {
    "question": "You heat a thick bowl of soup and find hot and cool spots. What would help make its temperature more even?",
    "options": [
      {
        "text": "Stir the soup and let it stand.",
        "isCorrect": true,
        "feedback": "Right. Stirring mixes hot and cool areas, and standing gives heat time to spread."
      },
      {
        "text": "Leave the soup untouched and eat it.",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "feedback": "Not quite. Without stirring or standing, the soup can still have cool spots."
      },
      {
        "text": "Move the bowl without stirring it.",
        "isCorrect": false,
        "feedback": "Not quite. Moving the bowl alone doesn’t mix the hot and cool soup."
      }
    ]
  },
  "recap": [
    "Microwaves enter food after bouncing around the oven.",
    "Their changing electric field moves water molecules, spreading energy as heat.",
    "Heating can be uneven, so stirring and standing help heat spread."
  ],
  "goFurther": {
    "overviewCourse": "Everyday Physics",
