{
  "title": "How compound interest works",
  "screens": [
    {
      "title": "A growing savings balance",
      "text": "Imagine you put $100 in a savings account and leave it there. The bank pays you interest: extra money for keeping your savings with it. What happens if you leave that extra money in the account, too?",
      "imagePrompt": "A savings account starting with $100, with a small amount of interest added to the balance."
    },
    {
      "title": "Your first year",
      "text": "Suppose the account pays 10% interest once a year. With no deposits or withdrawals, your $100 earns $10 in the first year. You now have $110. This is a simple example, not a typical savings rate.",
      "imagePrompt": "A savings balance of $100 with $10 of interest added, making a new balance of $110."
    },
    {
      "title": "Interest earns interest",
      "text": "In the second year, you earn 10% on the full $110, not just your original $100. That adds $11, bringing you to $121. Earning interest on earlier interest is called compound interest.",
      "imagePrompt": "A savings balance made of the original $100 and $10 of earlier interest, both contributing to $11 of new interest."
    },
    {
      "title": "It can grow debt, too",
      "text": "Compounding can also work against you. If unpaid interest is added to a loan or credit card balance, later interest may be charged on it, too. Payments can reduce what you owe; the account terms explain how charges work.",
      "imagePrompt": "A credit card balance with unpaid interest added, followed by a later interest charge based on the larger balance."
    },
